WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.838
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:09.181
It was a beautiful summer morning.

00:00:09.181 --> 00:00:12.363
I opened my eyes in the warm sunshine.

00:00:12.363 --> 00:00:15.230
And then, I walked to the restroom,
washed my face,

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.860
and looked at myself in the mirror,
and smiled.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.759
Then, I realized something was wrong.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:28.320
The person that meet her tried to smile,
but the smile was broken and cold.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:33.940
This was the day when I realized
half of my face was paralyzed.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.980
I lost the ability to move
the right side of my face.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.120
I could not blink, or
close my right eye, so

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.350
I always need to wear an eye patch,
during the day.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.540
And put tape, on my eye,
to fall asleep at night.

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.720
And there was always spit
dripping from my mouth.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:59.710
I visited a major hospitals in
South Korea, but there was no doctor

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:04.050
who could clearly tell me why I had
this illness and how to treat it.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:10.330
I was a 19 year old girl who loved
to put on makeup and dress up, but

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.520
the only thing I could do was
lie in the hospital bed and cry.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:22.570
I blamed my doctors in this illness,
and I blamed even myself.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.310
I was really devastated.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.740
And one day my mom asked me,
do you remember Urosoft anymore?

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.930
You know that fluffy seal?

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.420
Whenever you hugged it,
it made you feel better.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:41.045
Yeah, how can I forget my stuffed seal?

00:01:41.045 --> 00:01:43.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My parents gave it to me as a present

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.090
when I was three, and I really loved it.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.962
It was the most unique stuffed
animal in Korea at the time.

00:01:49.962 --> 00:01:53.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I still remember that it was

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.786
a kind of difficult to unwrap
this gift box by myself.

00:01:57.786 --> 00:02:01.900
Because that fluffy seal was as
tall as I was at three years old.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:08.375
And I also still remember how
excited I was when I finally

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:10.877
took off my seal from the box.

00:02:10.877 --> 00:02:13.830
At that moment,
this thought came to my mind.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:20.070
Maybe this illness is also one of
the gifts I received from my life,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.960
a gift, kind of ugly and crappy outside-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.490
&gt;&gt; But may have something

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.240
beautiful inside.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:31.870
And it is painful and

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:36.260
difficult to unwrap this gift because
I'm getting a really giant one.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:43.210
So from this moment I try to stop
complaining about this illness and

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.300
try to feel good about it.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.470
And as you can imagine it was not easy.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:55.490
I couldn’t stop thinking the, so let’s
say this is the gift, why am I the only

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.785
one getting these crappy gifts when my
friends are enjoying theirs on vacation?

00:02:58.785 --> 00:03:04.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I also asked myself questions like,

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.610
what if there's nothing
inside of this gift box?

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.330
What is the point of my suffering?

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:16.080
And the scariest part was,
what if my face stays paralyzed forever?

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.830
I struggled a lot,
many of the times were in vain.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.349
But the one thing I didn't give up was
thinking that there might be my stuff seal

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:30.190
inside of this painful gift.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:35.530
So I began thinking positive
sides of this illness.

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:38.130
And to my surprise,
there were at least a few.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.620
So my illness gave me,
not only a chance to rest, but

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.355
also an excuse to avoid my mom's nagging.

00:03:46.355 --> 00:03:49.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My mom couldn't say a word whenever

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.770
I overslept and
read a comic books in my bed.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.430
Also, I began varying things I took for
granted.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:01.230
My smile, my health, my friends and
family who care so much about me.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:05.180
After weeks of this practice,

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:09.180
my emotions were this illness,
evolved into gratefulness.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:14.310
It may sound really weird,
but I felt grateful for

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:18.705
having this facial paralysis as I did for
my stuff seal.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.190
In the meantime, my face which ended
up being healed by western medicine or

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.010
Chinese acupuncture,
begun showing in prevalence.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:34.070
And in the end, the right side on my
face came back to its original condition

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:35.130
without any side effects.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:39.670
I am looking at my gift box.

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:41.969
My gift box named facial paralysis.

00:04:41.969 --> 00:04:45.726
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And when I opened it up,

00:04:45.726 --> 00:04:51.155
one of the gifts inside was this new lens,
and when I looked through that lens,

00:04:51.155 --> 00:04:56.600
I could see not just blessings as a
blessings, but also burdens as blessings.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:59.180
There are people being grateful for

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:03.720
difficulties or hardships,
long after that event has passed.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.164
What I believe that seeing burdens as a
blessings, in the midst of those burdens,

00:05:08.164 --> 00:05:09.880
help need a lot to recover.

00:05:12.155 --> 00:05:16.640
But being grateful for
burdens, that's a lot of work.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.896
It took me weeks and a month to feel
generously grateful for this burden.

00:05:20.896 --> 00:05:23.060
So why should we bother?

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.017
It turns out there has been a lot of
research showing the positive effects of

00:05:28.017 --> 00:05:28.990
gratitude.

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:33.530
And being an ex business consultant
here like any other great consultant,

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.555
I categorize those impacts
into three focuses.

00:05:36.555 --> 00:05:39.836
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My favorite number, body, brain,

00:05:39.836 --> 00:05:40.580
and our emotions.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.350
Gratitude is good for our body.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.718
Many studies done by gratitude expert,

00:05:46.718 --> 00:05:51.175
Dr. which show that it leads
to 12% lower blood pressure.

00:05:51.175 --> 00:05:55.370
10% less stress about illnesses,
and more and

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.760
better sleep, all of which
are critical for curing illnesses.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.150
Also, it helps our brain, too.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.230
When you feel grateful, your brain
produces a surge of feel-good and

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:09.350
happy brain chemicals,
like dopamine or serotonin.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.150
Also, it is good for our emotion.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.450
Grateful people feel 25% happier and
less depressed.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:22.920
And more importantly, it makes us
emotionally resilient to hard times.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.470
Giving us the power to heal,
and bringing us hope.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.520
So now that we know that gratitude is
scientifically proven to be good for

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.650
us, so let's talk about how.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:38.803
It is relatively easy to say thank you for
blessings,

00:06:38.803 --> 00:06:44.629
such as free coffee at the shop, nice
weather at the a classmate's awesome talk.

00:06:44.629 --> 00:06:47.152
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:06:47.152 --> 00:06:50.690
things are getting tougher when
something bad things happen,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:56.240
like catching a cold or fighting with
their partner, getting a parking tickets.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:00.611
How can you feel grateful, for
getting a parking tickets?

00:07:00.611 --> 00:07:02.125
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:07:02.125 --> 00:07:07.260
here are some steps I suggest you as a
struggling to hone, the art of gratitude.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:11.000
So step one is, facing it.

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:13.830
You got the damn parking tickets.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Do not ignore your burden.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:17.800
It's not about self deception.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.710
And step two is breathing.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:27.210
Instead of saying shit or fuck-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.640
&gt;&gt; I love to say those words too, but

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.129
try to stop and
breathe to relieve your anger.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:38.043
And next, the most important step
is you need to make a choice.

00:07:38.043 --> 00:07:39.460
What choice?

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:42.310
You need to choose to be grateful for
this parking ticket.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:48.559
I know it's not easy but, in fact,
gratitude is all about choice.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.200
And fourth,
the most painful part is reflecting.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.040
Think about how this parking ticket
does something good for you.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.660
Imagine yourself holding the gift box

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.615
wrapped with all parking
tickets like this.

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:05.359
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What's inside of the box?

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.670
So for me, actually I don't have a car.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:14.480
So I ought to feel really grateful for
the fact that I had a car so

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:15.965
I could get the parking tickets.

00:08:15.965 --> 00:08:20.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Also we could be thankful for

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:23.056
the warning sign that tells
us do not park here again.

00:08:23.056 --> 00:08:24.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:27.865
prevents us from getting more expensive
parking tickets in the future.

00:08:27.865 --> 00:08:30.660
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.750
we just made a very tough choice to
be grateful for this parking ticket.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.814
But this choice gets even more
challenging when life throws really big

00:08:40.814 --> 00:08:42.169
gift boxes at you.

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:46.460
Like illness, loss,
the end of a relationship.

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:50.850
And honestly,
I am still struggling here, too.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:55.830
In my experience there is no quick fix.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:09:00.160
It takes endless training to
see burdens as blessings.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:04.449
But I hope my talk today helps you
see your own burdens as gifts.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:10.558
So then you can take out your own stuffed
animal, with joy, from those gift boxes.

00:09:10.558 --> 00:09:13.844
Thank you.

00:09:13.844 --> 00:09:14.958
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:14.958 --> 00:09:18.640
